PAGE TABLE MANAGEMENT
=====================

---- DATA STRUCTURES ----

>> A1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

/* 4 page state, identify what kind of supplemental page entry in the
    supplement page table */
enum page_status {
  ALL_ZERO,                       /* 0 */
  ON_FRAME,                       /* 1 */
  SWAP,                           /* 2 */
  FILE                            /* 3 */
};

/* Supplemental page table, contains a hash table, which stores
    supplemental page entry */
struct spt
  {
    struct hash page_map;
  };

/* Supplemental page table entry */
struct spt_elem
  {
    void *upage;                  /* User page */
    void *kpage;                  /* Kernel page */
    struct hash_elem elem;        /* Hash elem */
    bool dirty;                   /* Dirty page record */
    enum page_status status;      /* Record page state */

    uint32_t swap_id;             /* Record swap region index */

    /* Store file info*/
    struct file *file;
    off_t file_ofs;
    uint32_t read_bytes;
    uint32_t zero_bytes;
    bool writable;
  };

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: In a few paragraphs, describe your code for locating the frame,
>> if any, that contains the data of a given page.

At the beginning, we are given page (user vaddr). If it is mapped in page
directory of user page, we will return frame as a result. If no frame is
mapped to that page, we need to find the corresponding supplemental page
table entry from the supplemental page table.
Every 'struct spt_elem' contains an enum, which indicates status of the
page:

- ALL_ZERO: The page is not loaded, all contents are filled with zeros.
- ON_FRAME: It is loaded in frame. The address of the loaded frame is stored
            in kpage. In the frame table, you can find the corresponding
            frame table entry with key kpage.
- SWAP:     The current page is evicted from the frame and exists on the
            swap disk. At this time, swap disk, store in swap_id, and
            then use swap_id to read the appropriate part of disk later.

- FILE:     If the page is not in the frame, but need to be loaded from the
            file system. The location of a file, from offset to file, file_offset,
            read_bytes, zero_bytes, and so on are already stored in ept_elem.
            If writable is false, it is a read-only page.


>> A3: How does your code coordinate accessed and dirty bits between
>> kernel and user virtual addresses that alias a single frame, or
>> alternatively how do you avoid the issue?

We use pagedir_is_dirty () on three case: spt_elem is marked as dirty; the user
page is dirty; the kernel page is dirty; to find out whether it is dirty
because we are tracking dirty by page.



---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: When two user processes both need a new frame at the same time,
>> how are races avoided?

We use palloc_get_page() to allocate frame. palloc_get_page uses an
exclusive lock to make sure only one process get a free page index
at a time.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A5: Why did you choose the data structure(s) that you did for
>> representing virtual-to-physical mappings?

At this project, we need to implement lazy load. So it’s reasonable to
treat the page directory as supplemental page table and use user vaddr
as key of spt_elem which indicates user vaddr. Pages that are mapped with
a frame and currently in memory can be allocated through the original page
table, and the unloaded pages which include pages haven’t loaded from file
and pages have been swapped out can be found in supplemental page table.

