>> C2: Describe how memory mapped files integrate into your virtual
>> memory subsystem.  Explain how the page fault and eviction
>> processes differ between swap pages and other pages.

Each process keeps a list of memory mapped files, which is initialized when
starting a process. The mmap and munmap are system calls. mmap loads file
into memory and get a mapid while munmap frees the memory and check if
the relevant pages are dirty, and write back to the file if they are dirty.
When a process exits, it will free all its memory mapped files.

When allocating a frame, if page allocation fails, there is insufficient
memory. In this case, swapping is required. That is, we swap out a
frame page to a swap disk and assign a new page to that free space.

>> C3: Explain how you determine whether a new file mapping overlaps
>> any existing segment.

We get the file length and calculate pages that need before mapping.
Suppose it needs n pages. We check if uvaddr ... until
uvaddr + PGSIZE * (n - 1) have been mapped to frames.
We need to check if a frame is in PintOS frame table or spt table.
If any uvaddr is in the two page tables, return -1.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C4: Mappings created with "mmap" have similar semantics to those of
>> data demand-paged from executables, except that "mmap" mappings are
>> written back to their original files, not to swap.  This implies
>> that much of their implementation can be shared.  Explain why your
>> implementation either does or does not share much of the code for
>> the two situations.

We take the idea of sharing the data, but not sharing code.
So we only copy data to spt_elem.
A simple reason is that we are not good on sharing codes. So, in our
implementation, the best way is to duplicate the code, which is easy
to achieve.
Actually, sharing data won’t make any confusion because data are the 'same',
whick keeps consistency and reduces redundancy.

